#!args scenario=Scenario_OneRound seed=42
#
# Regression: RecycleCardChangeEvent must MOVE the card, never copy it.
#
# ExecuteAsync used to only do `DiscardedCardIds.Remove(CardId)` before adding the card to its
# destination. Every in-game caller recycles out of the discard pile, so that read as correct — but
# for any other source the original copy stayed where it was and the card ended up in two piles at
# once (here: hand AND draw deck). Nothing structurally forbids that, and a card in two piles counts
# as two cards everywhere — handsize/decksize conditions, ComputeHash, the card fans.
#
# `handsize` is what catches it: with the bug the deck grows and the hand never shrinks.
# `assert card 0 in deck` catches it too, because that subject reports the FIRST pile holding the
# card and checks hand before deck (see CliAssert.Read) — a duplicate still in hand reads as "hand".
#
# Values below are pinned to the seed in the #!args line above; they are deterministic given it.
# Run:  tests/run-cli.sh tests/qgc/recycle_moves_card.qgc

prompt                                  # turn 1, Germany, PLAY_CARD

assert card 0 in hand
assert handsize GERMANY == 7
assert decksize GERMANY == 26

# Deliberately recycling a card that is NOT in the discard pile — the case the old code duplicated.
api recycleCard --faction GERMANY --card 0 --destination TopOfDeck

assert card 0 in deck                   # FAILS ("hand") if the hand copy was left behind
assert handsize GERMANY == 6            # FAILS (7) if the hand copy was left behind
assert decksize GERMANY == 27
assert decktop GERMANY == 0

quit
